msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*The fifteen types of window, what each answers, its modes, and where it "
"appears.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Everything on the EMStudio screen is a **window**: an area of a given "
"type, with a header of its own. A workspace is an arrangement of windows "
"(see :ref:`reference-workspaces`); the same type can appear in several "
"workspaces, and two windows of the same type can show different things at"
" once."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A window's **type** says what it is. Its **mode**, where it has one, says"
" how it shows the same material. Both are chosen in the window's header."
msgstr ""

msgid "Working with windows"
msgstr ""

msgid "Change the type"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The menu at the left of the header (**Window type — transform this "
"window**) turns the window into another type, in the same place. It "
"offers **Graph**, **Narrative**, **Tabular**, **EMtree**, **Outliner**, "
"**Inspector**, **Viewer & card**, **Files**, **Shelf**, **Chronology**, "
"**3D scene**, **Study** and **Index**. The **Sheet** and the **Plugin "
"panel** are opened by their own workspace and by their plugin."
msgstr ""

msgid "Change the mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Mode** menu of the header (**Mode — what THIS window is showing**) "
"appears on the types that have modes. Each window keeps its own mode."
msgstr ""

msgid "Split and join"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Drag a corner of the area inwards to split it in two; drag it onto a "
"neighbour to join them. The same is in the type menu as **Split: a window"
" beside** and **Split: a window below**. The new half is a window of the "
"same type."
msgstr ""

msgid "Open in a new window"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Open in a new window**, in the type menu, opens a twin of the window in"
" a window of its own, which you can move to another screen. **Back into "
"the main window** returns it. In a browser the new window is a pop-up: if"
" the browser blocks it, allow pop-ups for the site of your node."
msgstr ""

msgid "Magnify"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Magnify this window** fills the workspace with it. Double-click the "
"empty part of the header, or press :kbd:`Ctrl+Space`; the same gesture "
"brings the arrangement back."
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "Close"
msgstr "Κλείσιμο"

msgid ""
"**Close window** removes the window from the workspace. The message that "
"follows offers **Reopen**."
msgstr ""

msgid "The header"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every header has three zones, always in the same places. At the left, "
"fixed: the window type and its **?**. In the centre, what the window "
"shows: its modes, its context (the sheet of a Tabular window, the epoch "
"of the 3D scene). At the right, its actions: filters, the search field, "
"``⋯`` and **⛶**. A header never scrolls sideways. When the window is too "
"narrow for it, the header gives way always in the same order: the labels "
"become icons (the name stays in the tooltip), then the verbs move into "
"``⋯``, listed under **In the bar (no room for them)**, then the search "
"field becomes a magnifier, and last the main command of the centre "
"narrows. In a very narrow window **Epochs** / **Horizons** become **E** /"
" **H**, and the modes and the **?** move into ``⋯`` too; there the **?** "
"becomes **What this window is**. ``⋯`` holds only what is not already in "
"the bar: when it has nothing, it is not shown."
msgstr ""

msgid "Menus and panels"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A menu or a panel that opens over the windows (a menu of the header, the "
"filter, the overlays of a canvas, **Visibility ▾**, **Colours ▾**, a "
"context menu) closes with a click outside it or with :kbd:`Esc`. The "
"click that closes does nothing else: it does not select the node under "
"it, nor open another menu; the next click works as usual. :kbd:`Esc` "
"closes the topmost panel only, so a submenu closes before its menu. "
"Pressing again the button that opened a panel closes it, and the focus "
"goes back to that button. A window where you have written something (a "
"dialog with a field, the form of a new document, **Copy the selection "
"into…**) does not close with a click outside: :kbd:`Esc` asks **Discard "
"what you wrote in this window?**, with **Keep writing** and **Discard**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A workspace you have reshaped can be restored to its arrangement of "
"origin (see :ref:`reference-workspaces`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "The types"
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "Graph"
msgstr "Γράφος"

msgid ""
"*What is there, in what order, and does it hold?* The canvas of the "
"study. Modes: **Matrix** (the stratigraphic matrix in epoch swimlanes, "
"laid out automatically), **Graph** (a free layout), **DTC** (the "
"transformation chain of the documentation) and **Graphs paradata** (one "
"node per open graph, with its own paradata and its relations with the "
"other graphs). It opens in **Matrix** in Stratigraphy, Sources, "
"Narrative, Space and Sheets; in Sheets it follows the unit of the sheet "
"with its neighbours. In Archive a Graph window is fixed on the chain of "
"the files and is called **Transformation chain (DTC)**: choose **Graph** "
"in its type menu to give it back its four modes."
msgstr ""

msgid "Outliner"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What is in the active graph?* The one tree of the study: the open files,"
" their graphs, and every dimension of a graph (units, epochs, documents, "
"models). It is the left column of every built-in workspace. A unit may "
"stand in more than one place of the tree (a document under several "
"epochs, say): the row you click stays selected and the list does not "
"scroll, and the unit's other rows are marked by a faint dotted underline,"
" their tooltip saying *The unit selected, in another place of the tree*. "
"In the **Sheets** workspace the Outliner lists the stratigraphic units "
"alone, the family the sheets describe, by epoch as elsewhere; a line at "
"its head says **Stratigraphic units —** and their number, and **Show "
"all** gives that window every dimension of the graph again (see :doc"
":`/how-to/sheets/fill-the-recording-sheet`)."
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "Inspector"
msgstr "Επιθεωρητής"

msgid ""
"*What is the selection?* The fields of the selected node, section by "
"section (see :ref:`reference-inspector`). It appears in Stratigraphy, "
"Sources, Narrative and Space."
msgstr ""

msgid "Viewer & card"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What does this point at?* The resource the current element points at, "
"shown as itself: an image, a PDF, a 3D document. The regions of an image "
"are traced here. Modes, offered when there are at least two images to lay"
" out: **Single** and **Gallery**. It is the main window of Sources, and "
"in Sheets it shows the photographs and documents of the unit."
msgstr ""

msgid "Files"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Which files do I have?* Where the bytes live. Modes: **Disk** (a folder "
"of this computer), **Bucket** (the configured object store, read as the "
"disk), **Graph's files** (the files the active graph cites), **In the "
"room** (the room's store), **Assets** (the study's assets, with their "
"versions), **Documents** (the tree of the document sets) and **Queue** "
"(the jobs of the processing queue). It is the main window of Archive, and"
" carries the card of the document picked in the chain."
msgstr ""

msgid "Shelf"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What does the study work from?* The curated list of resources a study "
"draws on, its own and others'. Modes: **List** and **Table**. It appears "
"in Archive, under the Files window."
msgstr ""

msgid "Tabular"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What do the rows say?* The study as tables. Its modes are sheets, in two"
" groups. **Sheets**, which can be edited: **Units**, **Epochs**, "
"**Documents**, **Claims**, **Authors**. **Computed views**, read only: "
"**Datings**, **Warnings**, **Models and proxies**, **Resources**, "
"**Interpretations**, **Combiners**. It appears in Space, on the sheet "
"**Models and proxies**."
msgstr ""

msgid "3D scene"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Where is it, and what shape does it have in each epoch?* The "
"representation models of an epoch and the proxies of its units, in one "
"scene you can orbit, with the epoch selector it shares with the "
"Chronology. Its bar holds, at the left, **Colours ▾** and **Visibility "
"▾**; in the centre, the epoch; at the right, the actions. The views "
"**Globe**, **Plan** and **Site** are in the column of round buttons under"
" the gizmo, and the hardware profile is a word of the line above the "
"scene. It is the main window of Space, and in Sheets it shows the proxy "
"of the unit."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sheet"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What does the recording sheet say of this unit?* The unit as its "
"recording sheet, drawn from a template, its boxes the graph's own fields."
" It is the main window of Sheets."
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "Narrative"
msgstr "Αφήγηση"

msgid ""
"*How do I tell it?* The page of the story, written and read in the same "
"window. It is the main window of Narrative."
msgstr ""

msgid "Index"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What does the story rest on?* The index of a story: its chapters, what "
"still needs writing, and how much of the graph the story covers. It is "
"the left column of Narrative, above the Outliner."
msgstr ""

msgid "Chronology"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Do the epochs hold?* The horizons of the project at its head, then the "
"epochs and phases as bars on a time axis, the overlaps with their "
"difference and their remedies, and the table of the bounds. It opens "
"below the Matrix with ``Tools ▸ Check the chronology…``, and from an "
"epoch's swimlane, its Inspector and the chronology warnings."
msgstr ""

msgid "Study"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What is this graph?* The graph's own facts: its name and identifier, the"
" metadata that its nodes inherit, the site position, the study and its "
"cultural context. It is reached by changing the type of a window."
msgstr ""

msgid "EMtree"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Which graphs are open, with which sources?* The files and graphs open "
"together, as one tree. The Outliner has taken its place in the built-in "
"workspaces; it is reached by changing the type of a window."
msgstr ""

msgid "Plugin panel"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*What does this plugin show?* The panel of an installed plugin, drawn by "
"EMStudio from the fields the plugin declares, under the plugin's own "
"name. It opens where the plugin proposes: beside, below, or in a window "
"of its own."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Log**, what happened, is a drawer of the status bar at the bottom "
"of the screen rather than a window."
msgstr ""

